   "source": [
    "### Goal: Audit Camden Council spending\n",
    "> This dataset contains **408,317 transactions**.\n",
    "> I used vectorised cleaning operations (Regex/Numpy logic) to ensure performance across a large-scale public sector dataset.\n",
    "> Please see the end of the project for my conclusions\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Data cleaning progress\n",
    "- [x] Missing values\n",
    "- [x] Consolidation of naming\n",
    "- [x] Investigation of outliers\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.  Missing values\n",
    "The raw data contained over 7,000 NaNs in `Irrecoverable VAT Amount GBP`. \n",
    "> **Decision:** Since the column otherwise consisted entirely of 0 values, I replaced the NaNs with 0.\n",
    "\n",
    "There was one other NaN value in `Beneficiary Name`.\n",
    "> **Decision:** I elected to drop the relevant row as it was statistically insignificant in the context of over 400,000 rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Consolidation of naming\n",
    "The raw data contained multiple variations for hidden identities, specifically `redacted` and `xxxxredactedxxxx`.\n",
    "> **Decision:** I used a Regular Expression `.*redacted.*` with the `case=False` flag. This ensures that any string containing \"redacted\" is captured regardless of padding characters or capitalisation, preventing the 'Volume of Spend' from being split across multiple rows.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. Statutory Levy Outliers\n",
    "During the `describe()` phase, a maximum value of £31.5m was identified.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Observation:** This transaction relates to the Greater London Authority (GLA). While it is a legitimate payment, its scale is three orders of magnitude larger than the mean. I have kept it in the primary DataFrame but will use a filtered subset for a supplier-specific visualisation to maintain a readable scale.\n"
   ]
  },
